			},
			"date": "2024-12-03T22:04:49.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@ardouglass\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>ardouglass</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> it actually is a business concept for some consulting agencies. I should maybe blog/give a ted talk on this. </p><p>I remember a conversation with one of my former supervisors which was like \"yes, you must deal with $framework, $subframework and $architecture even if it is over-engineered. Our business model foots on dealing with complex code. If it was simpler, the clients wouldn't reach out to us\" </p><p>Ideally, you deal with the complex code by making it simpler, but :D</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
